1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,403
How to tackle case PORTFLOW.

2
00:00:02,403 --> 00:00:07,022
A step-by-step method you can repeat on any case like this one.

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:21,004
Cases like this follow the operations & engineering improvement model: Baseline KPIs, then bottleneck/root cause, then optimize/simulate, then pilot, then ROI + KPIs.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:32,474
Start with one question: How does PortFlow reach <= 85% yard utilization and < 45-minute truck turns at +20% volume, within $10M?

5
00:00:32,474 --> 00:00:42,704
Split it into three branches with no overlap: Yard inventory (dwell), Yard handling (rehandles), Gate and landside (arrivals).

6
00:00:42,704 --> 00:00:45,952
Each person on the team owns one branch.

7
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:51,538
Within twenty minutes, write down a hypothesis you can prove wrong.

8
00:00:51,538 --> 00:00:57,632
Here: The core issue is fragmented release and schedule information, not a lack of space.

9
00:00:57,632 --> 00:01:09,341
Sharing it, booking trucks against ready containers and planning the yard around known pickups cuts dwell below 4.83 days and truck turn below 45 minutes without new land.

10
00:01:09,341 --> 00:01:12,012
If the data kills it, pivot and say so.

11
00:01:12,012 --> 00:01:13,518
Judges reward honesty.

12
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:18,033
Use only the tools that fit.

13
00:01:18,033 --> 00:01:23,679
Little's Law: inventory = throughput x dwell.

14
00:01:23,679 --> 00:01:28,071
Cause-versus-symptom tree (5 whys).

15
00:01:28,071 --> 00:01:34,971
Queueing view of truck arrivals: peak-to-average ratio.

16
00:01:34,971 --> 00:01:46,263
Then do the quick math: Today: 21,120 TEU / 6 days = 3,520 TEU/day; 21,120 / 24,000 = 88%.

17
00:01:46,263 --> 00:01:54,168
Year 5: 4,224 TEU/day x 6 days = 25,344 TEU = 106% of capacity.

18
00:01:55,170 --> 00:02:03,687
Score all three options with explicit weights: target attainment, return, payback, downside resilience and budget headroom.

19
00:02:03,687 --> 00:02:07,842
The budget is a hard gate: an option over budget cannot win.

20
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:11,148
Then build a solution ladder.

21
00:02:11,148 --> 00:02:13,137
Level one, the quick win.

22
00:02:13,137 --> 00:02:17,355
Level two, the cheapest option that meets the target.

23
00:02:17,355 --> 00:02:19,981
Level three, the value maximiser.

24
00:02:19,981 --> 00:02:21,891
Level four, the stretch.

25
00:02:21,891 --> 00:02:26,984
Your recommendation should explain where it sits on this ladder.

26
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:31,076
Finally, protect your time.

27
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:34,171
0:00 to 0:25, Read & align.

28
00:02:34,171 --> 00:02:37,038
0:25 to 0:55, Quick math.

29
00:02:37,038 --> 00:02:40,707
0:55 to 1:40, Package & finance.

30
00:02:40,707 --> 00:02:44,146
1:40 to 2:30, Deck (8 slides).

31
00:02:44,146 --> 00:02:47,471
2:30 to 3:00, Rehearse & Q&A.

32
00:02:47,471 --> 00:02:53,204
Video three shows the answer this method produces.
